15:27:37 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's a great question—it's natural to be skeptical because we almost never see rainbows from street lamps. But yes, artificial lights *can* create rainbows under the right conditions, though they look very different from sunlit rainbows. Let me explain with a few real-world examples and why they're so rare. ### When have people actually seen artificial-light rainbows? - **"Moonbows"** (rainbows from moonlight) are well-documented—they occur at night near waterfalls or during rain, but they appear nearly white to our eyes because moonlight is so dim. A long-exposure camera can reveal their colors. - **Artificial "fog bows"** – On a foggy night, a bright streetlamp can produce a faint, colorless circular ring of light around it (called a "glory" or "halo") if you stand in the right spot and look toward the lamp through fog. The colors are usually too weak for our eyes to see, but cameras can sometimes catch them. - **Garden sprinklers** – If you stand with your back to a bright outdoor floodlight and spray water from a garden hose, you can sometimes see a tiny rainbow arc. People have done this as a demonstration. ### So why don't we see them from ordinary street lamps all the time? 1. **You need the light *behind* you** – For a rainbow to form, the light must shine from behind you into water droplets in front of you. When you're walking under a streetlamp, the lamp is overhead or in front of you, not behind. You would have to stand with your back to the lamp, and the water droplets must be exactly opposite it—a rare alignment. 2. **Brightness and color** – Even a powerful streetlamp is millions of times dimmer than the sun. Our eyes' color sensors (cones) don't work well in dim light, so the rainbow's red, green, and blue bands vanish into gray. Only very bright artificial lights (like stadium lights or searchlights) can occasionally produce faint colors. 3. **Parallel vs. diverging light** – This is the biggest difference. Sunlight comes in parallel rays, so every raindrop gets the same angle of light, creating a huge consistent arc. A streetlamp's light spreads out in all directions. The rainbow it creates is tiny, localized, and only visible from a very specific viewing point. It's more like a small halo around the lamp than a giant arch in the sky. ### In short: You're right that 99.9% of the rainbows we see come from the sun. But artificial lights *can* make rainbows—they're just so faint, small, and hard to spot that most people never encounter one. So your intuition ("rainbows are from the sun") is correct for everyday experience, but the physics allows other light sources to do the same thing under special conditions. If you want to try seeing one yourself, wait for a foggy night, stand under a very bright streetlamp, and look *away* from the lamp at an angle—you might spot a faint, colorless ring. Or use a powerful flashlight and a misting spray bottle in a dark room. It won't be as dramatic as a solar rainbow, but it's real!
